{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01F1W8P2EAknpSdYGw73XdZz","type":"tool_result","content":"<persisted-output>\nOutput too large (169.7KB). Full output saved to: /Users/vivekgade/.claude/projects/-private-var-folders-fj-tl69n2xd067clrqbx31xtrtr0000gn-T-t13-6o4o32at-build-repo--lattice-rebuilds-t13-cyclops-b1-19c0ae/25e55146-d7ad-4b00-9b04-b33f1b7f87c0/tool-results/bzsich2i3.txt\n\nPreview (first 2KB):\n\"\"\"Read-only, cached access to the artifact tree for the viewer (spec §5).\n\n`Store` is the whole data layer of the UI. Three rules define it:\n\n**It only reads.** Nothing here writes, creates a directory, or recomputes a\npublished number. The one exception is explicitly labelled: the analog forward\npaths in `memory()` are re-derived from `nq_1h.close_adj` for display, and the\npayload carries `derived: true` saying so.\n\n**A missing artifact is data, not an exception.** Every method returns\n``{\"exists\": false, \"hint\": \"run `cyclops <cmd>`\"}`` when the file it needs has\nnot been built, so a half-built repo renders as a page of honest empty states\ninstead of a stack trace. Only genuinely broken input (a corrupt parquet)\nraises.\n\n**Everything it returns is strict JSON.** `json.dumps(payload,\nallow_nan=False)` must accept every payload: NaN/Inf become `null`, numpy\nscalars become Python scalars, and timestamps become ISO-8601 strings in\nAmerica/New_York *with the UTC offset* (`2024-03-01T10:00:00-05:00`), so the\nfront end never has to guess a zone.\n\n**The live tail is an overlay, never a substitute.** The batch artifacts\n(`data/processed/nq_{rung}.parquet`, `data/regimes/*`) are rebuilt once a night\nat 17:15 ET; between two of those the live loop publishes the same rungs' bars\nand labels into `data/live/bars_{rung}.parquet` and\n`data/live/labels_{rung}.parquet`. Every series this module serves is the batch\ntable with the live rows *strictly past its last stamp* concatenated onto it\n(`_overlay`), and each of those rows carries `live: true` so the screen can mark\nwhere the record ends and the tail begins. Nothing is recomputed to make that\njoin: the live path runs the same `resample_bars` and the same rules labeller\nover a shorter window (spec §12.1), which is what makes the two halves the same\nseries. What the live half does *not* carry is said with nulls rather than\nfilled in — the HMM columns, the state memory and the raw rule inputs are\n...\n</persisted-output>","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"25e55146-d7ad-4b00-9b04-b33f1b7f87c0","uuid":"d1f878a0-1ca7-4e61-8462-1c43635b3358","timestamp":"2026-10-31T12:04:21.957Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"\"\"\"Read-only, cached access to the artifact tree for the viewer (spec §5).\n\n`Store` is the whole data layer of the UI. Three rules define it:\n\n**It only reads.** Nothing here writes, creates a directory, or recomputes a\npublished number. The one exception is explicitly labelled: the analog forward\npaths in `memory()` are re-derived from `nq_1h.close_adj` for display, and the\npayload carries `derived: true` saying so.\n\n**A missing artifact is data, not an exception.** Every method returns\n``{\"exists\": false, \"hint\": \"run `cyclops <cmd>`\"}`` when the file it needs has\nnot been built, so a half-built repo renders as a page of honest empty states\ninstead of a stack trace. Only genuinely broken input (a corrupt parquet)\nraises.\n\n**Everything it returns is strict JSON.** `json.dumps(payload,\nallow_nan=False)` must accept every payload: NaN/Inf become `null`, numpy\nscalars become Python scalars, and timestamps become ISO-8601 strings in\nAmerica/New_York *with the UTC offset* (`2024-03-01T10:00:00-05:00`), so the\nfront end never has to guess a zone.\n\n**The live tail is an overlay, never a substitute.** The batch artifacts\n(`data/processed/nq_{rung}.parquet`, `data/regimes/*`) are rebuilt once a night\nat 17:15 ET; between two of those the live loop publishes the same rungs' bars\nand labels into `data/live/bars_{rung}.parquet` and\n`data/live/labels_{rung}.parquet`. Every series this module serves is the batch\ntable with the live rows *strictly past its last stamp* concatenated onto it\n(`_overlay`), and each of those rows carries `live: true` so the screen can mark\nwhere the record ends and the tail begins. Nothing is recomputed to make that\njoin: the live path runs the same `resample_bars` and the same rules labeller\nover a shorter window (spec §12.1), which is what makes the two halves the same\nseries. What the live half does *not* carry is said with nulls rather than\nfilled in — the HMM columns, the state memory and the raw rule inputs are\nnightly-only (spec §12.6), and `bar_detail` names that instead of dashing\nsilently.\n\nCaching is per file, keyed on `(mtime_ns, size)`. Re-running `cyclops eod`\nunder a live server is picked up on the next request without a restart, and a\ndeleted artifact reverts to the \"not built yet\" state. A union is cached on\n*both* its files' stats, so a scheduler tick invalidates it the same way.\n\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport json\nimport math\nfrom datetime import date, datetime\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nimport numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nimport pyarrow.parquet as pq\n\nfrom cyclops import config\nfrom cyclops.config import LABEL_RUNGS, MEMORY_HORIZONS, RUNGS, TZ\nfrom cyclops.data.store import META_KEY, read_parquet\nfrom cyclops.patterns import formations as pattern_formations\nfrom cyclops.patterns import paths as pattern_paths\nfrom cyclops.patterns import similarity as pattern_similarity\nfrom cyclops.patterns import structure as pattern_structure\nfrom cyclops.patterns.admissibility import surfaced as _surfaced\nfrom cyclops.live import stream as live_stream\nfrom cyclops.regime import rungs as rung_paths\nfrom cyclops.ui.decimate import line_thin, ohlc_buckets\n\n__all__ = [\"Store\", \"jsonable\", \"iso_et\"]\n\n#: Default point budget for a `bars` range query.\nDEFAULT_MAX_POINTS = 3000\n\n#: Horizon (in 1h bars) of the analog forward paths `memory()` derives.\nFWD_BARS = MEMORY_HORIZONS[\"1d\"]\n\n#: The one rung a transition model, an HMM challenger and a state memory were\n#: ever fitted for. Mirrored from `cyclops.live.labels.TRANSITION_RUNG` rather\n#: than imported, so the read-only viewer does not drag the live path's model\n#: stack (joblib, scikit-learn) into every server start; `tests/ui/test_store.py`\n#: asserts the two agree.\nTRANSITION_RUNG = \"1h\"\n\n#: The three trend-strength inputs and the config thresholds they are read\n#: against (`cyclops.regime.rules.structure_votes`).\n_TREND_INPUTS = (\n    (\"ts_adx_14\", \"adx_hi\", \"adx_lo\"),\n    (\"ts_hurst_128\", \"hurst_hi\", \"hurst_lo\"),\n    (\"ts_er_20\", \"er_hi\", \"er_lo\"),\n)\n_DIR_INPUTS = (\"dir_ema50_slope_5\", \"dir_di_diff_14\", \"dir_swing_50\")\n_VOL_INPUTS = (\"vol_rv_cc_20_pct\", \"vol_atr_pct_14_pct\")\n\n#: The 15m change-of-behaviour columns, shipped beside the `ctx4h_`/`ctx1d_`\n#: block. They are not a 15m regime reading -- there is none -- they are the\n#: inputs the 1h transition-risk model reads out of the four 15m bars inside\n#: the hour, and the Explain screen says exactly that beside them.\n_M15_INPUTS = (\n    \"m15_vol_pct_jump_4\", \"m15_er_drop_4\", \"m15_swing_breaks_4\",\n    \"m15_dir_disagree\", \"m15_n_bars\",\n)\n\n#: Columns of the regimes artifact the ribbon and the reading panel need.\n_REGIME_COLS = (\n    \"regime\", \"direction\", \"vol_band\", \"transitional\", \"bars_in_regime\",\n    \"hazard_5\", \"expected_remaining_median\", \"transition_risk\", \"hmm_regime\",\n    \"disagree\", \"config_version\",\n)\n\n#: Columns of a rung's feature table that are not plottable series: identifiers,\n#: bookkeeping and the session stamp. Everything else is a number the labeller\n#: read, and every one of those is offered.\nFEATURE_EXCLUDE = frozenset({\n    \"session_date\", \"roll_date\", \"active_iid\", \"n_1m\", \"rung\", \"config_version\",\n    \"symbol\", \"instrument_id\",\n})\n\n#: Per-bar price fields, as `{payload key: artifact column}`.\n_BAR_FIELDS = {\n    \"o\": \"open\", \"h\": \"high\", \"l\": \"low\", \"c\": \"close\", \"v\": \"volume\",\n    \"o_adj\": \"open_adj\", \"h_adj\": \"high_adj\", \"l_adj\": \"low_adj\",\n    \"c_adj\": \"close_adj\",\n}\n\n#: Which `cyclops` command builds what, for the \"not built yet\" hints.\nCMD_BUILD = \"build\"\nCMD_FEATURES = \"features\"\nCMD_REGIMES = \"regimes\"\nCMD_EOD = \"eod\"\nCMD_CONFIGS = \"configs list\"\nCMD_PARITY = \"live-parity\"\nCMD_RUNG_REGIMES = \"rung-regimes\"\nCMD_SCHEDULER = \"scheduler\"\nCMD_LIVE = \"live\"\nCMD_PATTERNS = \"patterns\"\n\n#: The columns `/api/patterns` publishes for each detection, grouped as the\n#: reading panel reads them: what fired, what the market was doing, whether the\n#: context admits the reading, and whether the confirmation question has been\n#: settled. `confirm_known_at` rides with `confirmed` and is never separated from\n#: it — a confirmation without the stamp at which it became knowable is exactly\n#: the look-ahead §16.7 exists to prevent.\n_PATTERN_COLS = (\n    \"pattern\", \"rung\", \"side\", \"family\", \"n_bars\", \"significance\", \"prior_trend\",\n    \"rank\", \"pattern_high\", \"pattern_low\",\n    \"regime\", \"direction\", \"vol_band\", \"structure\", \"transitional\",\n    \"label_ts\", \"label_staleness_min\",\n    \"swing_trend\", \"location\", \"zone_id\", \"zone_kind\", \"zone_dist_atr\",\n    \"line_id\", \"line_kind\", \"line_dist_atr\",\n    \"context_class\", \"admissible\", \"admissible_reason\", \"cell_id\",\n    \"confirm_ts\", \"confirm_known_at\", \"confirm_bars\",\n)\n\n#: What `/api/formations` publishes for each row: the artifact's own column\n#: contract, in the artifact's own order, and nothing narrowed away. A formation\n#: table is ONE grain — one row per shape — so unlike the structure long form\n#: there is no union of four object kinds for a consumer to meet, and picking a\n#: subset here would only be a second opinion about which of the engine's columns\n#: matter.\n#:\n#: The context half is MIRRORED from `patterns.pipeline.FORMATION_CONTEXT_COLUMNS`\n#: rather than imported, for the reason `TRANSITION_RUNG` is mirrored from\n#: `live.labels`: the read-only viewer must not drag the build pipeline (the\n#: calibration grid, the validation windows) into every server start.\n#: `tests/ui/test_store.py` asserts the two lists agree, so they cannot drift.\n_FORMATION_CONTEXT_COLS = (\n    \"regime\", \"direction\", \"vol_band\", \"structure\", \"transitional\",\n    \"label_ts\", \"label_staleness_min\", \"swing_trend\", \"location\",\n    \"context_class\", \"cell_id\",\n)\n_FORMATION_COLS = tuple(pattern_formations.FORMATION_COLUMNS) + _FORMATION_CONTEXT_COLS\n\n#: The columns §23.2 quarantines behind the confirmation's own stamp, imported\n#: from the engine rather than restated so the viewer cannot quarantine a\n#: different set than the one the engine wrote. A row whose `confirm_known_at`\n#: is later than the rung's as-of comes back with every one of these `None` and\n#: `confirmation_settled: false` — the artifact is allowed to know how the break\n#: went, because it is a full-history table and the break is a fact about a later\n#: bar, and the screen is not (§16.7).\n_FORMATION_QUARANTINE = pattern_formations.CONFIRMATION_QUARANTINE\n\n#: The four numbers a t15 cell contributes, and the only four. The verdict is\n#: the pre-registered judgement; `hit_rate`/`base_rate`/`n` are what it was made\n#: from, because a PASS on 41 detections and a PASS on 4,100 are different\n#: sentences and the screen must not print them identically.\n_T15_FIELDS = (\"verdict\", \"hit_rate\", \"base_rate\", \"n\")\n\n#: What to say when `data/validation/{split}/t15.json` is not there. The browser\n#: NEVER fills these in: a verdict computed in the viewer would be a fresh look\n#: at a held-out split, taken at page load, by nobody's decision (§19).\nT15_HINT = (\"run `cyclops validate --split validation` — the cell verdicts come \"\n            \"from data/validation/validation/t15.json and are never computed here\")\n\n#: What a formation's `b_origin` means, said once and carried on the payload so\n#: no client has to compose the sentence itself. A `*_m`/`*_b` pair is a boundary\n#: in `price = m * bar + b` with `bar` counted from the first bar of the window\n#: that FITTED it, so a `b` is a coordinate rather than a price and means nothing\n#: without its origin. Two rows may be compared exactly when both name the same\n#: non-null origin; a null names no origin and is comparable with nothing.\nB_ORIGIN_CAUTION = (\n    \"`b_origin` names the bar origin a row's `*_b` intercepts are counted from. \"\n    \"Two rows' intercepts may be compared only when both carry the SAME non-null \"\n    \"`b_origin`; a null origin is comparable with nothing, not even another null. \"\n    \"Evaluate a boundary only at bars inside that row's own `e*_ts` span.\")\n\n#: The origin every row of the nightly artifact shares: bar 0 of\n#: `nq_{rung}.parquet`, because the batch build fits over the whole history in\n#: one pass. Live-tail rows get `None` instead — see `_formation_rows`.\n_B_ORIGIN_BATCH = \"batch\"\n\n#: How many stamps one `/api/bars/{rung}/windows` call may ask for, and how many\n#: bars each of those windows may hold. A snapshot strip draws one thumbnail per\n#: similar occurrence, and `patterns_similar` is itself capped at\n#: `PATTERN_SIM_MAX_K` = 50 — but a *thumbnail* is forty bars, so the batch route\n#: is capped at what a strip actually renders rather than at what the similarity\n#: route can return. Past that it is a bulk export of the bar table wearing a\n#: strip's clothes, which is the same refusal `/api/patterns` already makes.\nMAX_WINDOW_STAMPS = 24\nMAX_WINDOW_BARS = 120\n\n#: What `bar_windows` publishes per bar, as `{payload key: artifact column}`.\n#: BACK-ADJUSTED only, and the payload says so: a thumbnail of 2016 drawn from\n#: raw prices and one of 2026 drawn from raw prices are not on one scale, and the\n#: adjusted series is the one every feature and every detection was computed on.\n_WINDOW_FIELDS = {\"o\": \"open_adj\", \"h\": \"high_adj\", \"l\": \"low_adj\", \"c\": \"close_adj\"}\n\n#: The context columns a similarity hit carries, and the query carries too --\n#: the similarity module's own list, imported rather than restated, so the served\n#: payload and the in-process one cannot drift into two vocabularies. `confirmed`\n#: is published beside these but is not one of them: it comes from the hit, where\n#: the causality rule already settled it.\n_SIM_CONTEXT_COLS = pattern_similarity.CONTEXT_KEYS\n\n#: The hint `live()` hands back when the loop has never published. It names both\n#: ways of starting it: the bare command for a checkout, the compose service for\n#: the deployment the spec (§13.4) describes.\nLIVE_HINT = \"run `cyclops scheduler` (or `docker compose up -d scheduler`)\"\n\n#: The per-rung label columns `_rung_at` publishes. Deliberately the label and\n#: its age only: the per-rung tables carry no hazard model and no transition\n#: risk, and dashing a column that does not exist is honester than inventing it.\n_RUNG_LABEL_COLS = (\"regime\", \"direction\", \"vol_band\", \"transitional\",\n                    \"bars_in_regime\")\n\n#: Where this bar came from, and the two things that half genuinely cannot\n#: answer. Shipped on every `bar_detail` for a live bar so the screen can say it\n#: rather than let the reader guess.\n#:\n#: It used to name the rule inputs too. It does not any more, and that is a\n#: statement about the artifact rather than about the wording: labels schema v3\n#: publishes them, so the why-table on a live bar reads as an argument, and a\n#: note still claiming they were withheld would contradict the table under it.\n#: The HMM and the state memory stay on the list because they really are absent\n#: — Viterbi is a joint path over a whole sequence and the memory is a past-only\n#: kNN over the full index, and neither has a per-bar form to publish (§12.6).\nLIVE_GAP_NOTE = (\n    \"This bar comes from the live loop, past the last nightly build. The label, \"\n    \"the votes and the values behind them are published live; the HMM challenger \"\n    \"and the state memory are not — those are rebuilt over the whole history and \"\n    \"fill in at the 17:15 ET (16:15 CT) nightly chain (spec §12.6).\"\n)\n\n\n# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\n# JSON coercion\n# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\ndef iso_et(ts) -> str | None:\n    \"\"\"ISO-8601 in America/New_York *with offset*, or None for NaT.\"\"\"\n    if ts is None:\n        return None\n    ts = pd.Timestamp(ts)\n    if ts is pd.NaT or pd.isna(ts):\n        return None\n    ts = ts.tz_localize(TZ) if ts.tz is None else ts.tz_convert(TZ)\n    return ts.isoformat()\n\n\ndef _iso_date(ts) -> str | None:\n    \"\"\"`YYYY-MM-DD` for a session/roll date, or None.\"\"\"\n    if ts is None:\n        return None\n    ts = pd.Timestamp(ts)\n    if ts is pd.NaT or pd.isna(ts):\n        return None\n    return str(ts.date())\n\n\ndef jsonable(obj):\n    \"\"\"Coerce anything an artifact can hold into strict-JSON-able Python.\n\n    NaN, Inf, NaT and pandas NA all become `None` -- `json.dumps` will happily\n    emit a bare `NaN`, which no strict parser reads back, and the viewer's\n    whole job is showing gaps honestly rather than as zeros.\n    \"\"\"\n    if obj is None or obj is pd.NaT or obj is pd.NA:\n        return None\n    if isinstance(obj, str):\n        return str(obj)\n    if isinstance(obj, (bool, np.bool_)):\n        return bool(obj)\n    if isinstance(obj, (int, np.integer)):\n        return int(obj)\n    if isinstance(obj, (float, np.floating)):\n        v = float(obj)\n        return v if math.isfinite(v) else None\n    if isinstance(obj, (pd.Timestamp, datetime)):\n        return iso_et(obj)\n    if isinstance(obj, date):\n        return obj.isoformat()\n    if isinstance(obj, pd.Timedelta):\n        return obj.isoformat()\n    if isinstance(obj, dict):\n        return {str(k): jsonable(v) for k, v in obj.items()}\n    if isinstance(obj, np.ndarray):\n        return [jsonable(v) for v in obj.tolist()]\n    if isinstance(obj, pd.Series):\n        return {str(k): jsonable(v) for k, v in obj.items()}\n    if isinstance(obj, (list, tuple, set, frozenset)):\n        return [jsonable(v) for v in obj]\n    try:\n        if pd.isna(obj):\n            return None\n    except (TypeError, ValueError):\n        pass\n    return str(obj)\n\n\ndef _stat_key(path: Path) -> tuple[int, int] | None:\n    \"\"\"`(mtime_ns, size)`, or `None` for a file that is not there.\n\n    `None` is a *value*, not a failure: \"the live loop has not published this\n    rung yet\" has to invalidate the cache the moment it does.\n    \"\"\"\n    try:\n        st = path.stat()\n    except OSError:\n        return None\n    return (st.st_mtime_ns, st.st_size)\n\n\ndef _et_index(values) -> pd.DatetimeIndex:\n    \"\"\"Any timestamp column as a tz-aware ET `DatetimeIndex`.\"\"\"\n    idx = pd.DatetimeIndex(values)\n    return idx.tz_localize(TZ) if idx.tz is None else idx.tz_convert(TZ)\n\n\ndef _row_stamps(df: pd.DataFrame) -> pd.Series:\n    \"\"\"When each row of a `ts`-column frame happened: `ts`, else `valid_from`.\n\n    A detection has a `ts`; a zone version and a trend line have a `valid_from`\n    and no `ts` at all. One rule, so the live/batch overlay and the window filter\n    below cannot disagree about which stamp a row is judged by.\n    \"\"\"\n    ts = (pd.Series(_et_index(df[\"ts\"]), index=df.index)\n          if \"ts\" in df.columns else pd.Series(pd.NaT, index=df.index, dtype=\"object\"))\n    if \"valid_from\" in df.columns:\n        vf = pd.Series(_et_index(df[\"valid_from\"]), index=df.index)\n        ts = ts.where(ts.notna(), vf)\n    return ts\n\n\ndef _formation_stamps(df: pd.DataFrame) -> pd.Series:\n    \"\"\"When each formation row happened, for the live/batch overlay: `known_at`.\n\n    NOT `ts`, which is what `_row_stamps` would pick. A formation's `ts` is its\n    FIRST defining extremum — the bar the shape starts on — and the shape did not\n    exist then. A head and shoulders that started three weeks ago and completed\n    this afternoon carries a `ts` far behind the nightly artifact's last one, so\n    an overlay keyed on `ts` would drop every formation the live tail found and\n    the tail would publish nothing between two 17:15 chains.\n\n    `known_at` is the stamp the whole formation vocabulary is written against:\n    the context is joined at it (`pipeline.formation_context`), the live tail\n    emits on it (`patterns_tail.emitted_formations`) and the API window is\n    filtered by it below. One stamp, in every place a formation is compared.\n    \"\"\"\n    return pd.Series(_et_index(df[\"known_at\"]), index=df.index)\n\n\ndef _span_mask(part: pd.DataFrame, kind: str, lo, hi) -> pd.Series:\n    \"\"\"Which structure objects are VALID inside `[lo, hi]`.\n\n    A zone version, a trend line and a range band all have a span: they are in\n    the window when they began at or before its end and had not ended before its\n    start, with an open `valid_to` reading as \"still live\" rather than as \"ends\n    at NaT\". A swing is a point and is in when it happened, or when it became\n    knowable, inside it. One branch, three kinds: a range band is a band with a\n    span, so it needs the same rule and not a fourth copy of it.\n\n    A formation has a span too, and it is spelled with different column names --\n    `known_at` to `confirm_known_at` rather than `valid_from` to `valid_to` --\n    which is the whole reason it gets a branch instead of a fourth copy of the\n    same three lines.\n\n    The distinction is the whole reason this function exists. Filtering an object\n    with a span by its creation stamp drops exactly the levels a chart most needs\n    at its right edge — the old ones that keep getting touched.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not len(part):\n        return pd.Series([], dtype=bool, index=part.index)\n    keep = pd.Series(True, index=part.index)\n    if kind == \"formation\":\n        # A formation HAS a span: it exists from `known_at` and is finished when\n        # its confirmation (or its expiry) settled. Serving it on its creation\n        # stamp alone would drop exactly the shapes a chart most needs at its\n        # right edge -- the ones that formed last month and broke out this week.\n        frm = _et_index(part[\"known_at\"])\n        to = _et_index(part[\"confirm_known_at\"])\n        keep = pd.Series(True, index=part.index)\n        if hi is not None:\n            keep &= np.asarray(frm <= hi)\n        if lo is not None:\n            keep &= np.asarray(to.isna()) | np.asarray(to >= lo)\n        return keep\n    if kind == \"swing\":\n        at = _et_index(part[\"ts\"])\n        known = _et_index(part[\"known_at\"])\n        if lo is not None:\n            keep &= np.asarray(at >= lo) | np.asarray(known >= lo)\n        if hi is not None:\n            keep &= np.asarray(at <= hi) | np.asarray(known <= hi)\n        return keep\n    frm, to = _et_index(part[\"valid_from\"]), _et_index(part[\"valid_to\"])\n    if hi is not None:\n        keep &= np.asarray(frm <= hi)\n    if lo is not None:\n        keep &= np.asarray(to.isna()) | np.asarray(to >= lo)\n    return keep\n\n\ndef _test_number(path: Path) -> tuple[int, str]:\n    \"\"\"Sort key for a `t<N>.json` filename: numerically, so t9 precedes t10.\n\n    A non-numeric `t*.json` sorts last under its own name rather than raising —\n    the directory is an artifact tree, not a schema, and a file nobody planned\n    for must not take the validation screen down.\n    \"\"\"\n    digits = path.stem[1:]\n    return (int(digits), \"\") if digits.isdigit() else (1 << 30, path.stem)\n\n\ndef _missing(cmd: str, **extra) -> dict:\n    \"\"\"The universal \"this artifact has not been built yet\" payload.\"\"\"\n    return {\"exists\": False, \"hint\": f\"run `cyclops {cmd}`\", **extra}\n\n\n# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\n# Store\n# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\n#: Every rung this repo builds, FINEST first. Used wherever \"the most recent\n#: price\" or \"show the band rungs in a sensible order\" is meant: `config.RUNGS`\n#: is the LIVE-PATH rung set and excludes 5m/30m, so it is the wrong key for\n#: ordering anything that can include a research rung.\n_RUNG_ORDER = (\"1m\", \"5m\", \"15m\", \"30m\", \"1h\", \"4h\", \"1d\")\n\n#: How old the newest 1m print may be and still be called live, in minutes.\n#: Generous enough to survive a thin overnight minute with no trade, tight\n#: enough that a stream which died an hour ago stops claiming to be live and\n#: falls back to the nightly close with its real age attached.\nSTREAM_QUOTE_MAX_AGE_MIN = 15.0\n\n\nclass Store:\n    \"\"\"Cached, read-only reader over one artifact root (a repo checkout).\n\n    `root` is the repository root: `<root>/data/...` plus `<root>/docs/journal`.\n    Defaults to `cyclops.config.ROOT`, i.e. the real tree.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, root: Path | str | None = None) -> None:\n        self.root = Path(root) if root is not None else Path(config.ROOT)\n        data = self.root / \"data\"\n        self.processed_dir = data / \"processed\"\n        self.features_dir = data / \"features\"\n        self.regimes_dir = data / \"regimes\"\n        self.memory_dir = data / \"state_memory\"\n        self.validation_dir = data / \"validation\"\n        self.dq_dir = data / \"dq\"\n        self.eod_dir = data / \"eod\"\n        self.live_dir = data / \"live\"\n        self.patterns_dir = data / \"patterns\"\n        self.vol_dir = data / \"vol\"\n        self.configs_dir = self.regimes_dir / \"configs\"\n        self.journal_dir = self.root / \"docs\" / \"journal\"\n\n        self._cache: dict[Path | str, tuple[object, object]] = {}\n        self._reads = 0\n        self._hits = 0\n\n    # -- caching ------------------------------------------------------------\n    def cache_stats(self) -> dict:\n        \"\"\"`{\"reads\", \"hits\", \"entries\"}` -- for tests and `--check` output.\"\"\"\n        return {\"reads\": self._reads, \"hits\": self._hits, \"entries\": len(self._cache)}\n\n    def _load(self, path: Path, loader, cache_key: Path | str | None = None):  # noqa: D401\n        \"\"\"Cached `loader(path)`, invalidated when (mtime, size) changes.\n\n        `cache_key` lets two different views of the same file (the full frame\n        and the footer-only summary) live side by side in the cache while\n        still being invalidated by that one file's stat.\n        \"\"\"\n        ck = path if cache_key is None else cache_key\n        try:\n            st = path.stat()\n        except OSError:\n            self._cache.pop(ck, None)\n            return None\n        key = (st.st_mtime_ns, st.st_size)\n        hit = self._cache.get(ck)\n        if hit is not None and hit[0] == key:\n            self._hits += 1\n            return hit[1]\n        obj = loader(path)\n        self._reads += 1\n        self._cache[ck] = (key, obj)\n        return obj\n\n    def _parquet(self, path: Path) -> pd.DataFrame | None:\n        got = self._load(path, lambda p: read_parquet(p))\n        return None if got is None else got[0]\n\n    def _parquet_meta(self, path: Path) -> dict | None:\n        got = self._load(path, lambda p: read_parquet(p))\n        return None if got is None else got[1]\n\n    def _json(self, path: Path):\n        return self._load(path, lambda p: json.loads(p.read_text()))\n\n    def _json_or_error(self, path: Path) -> tuple[object | None, str | None]:\n        \"\"\"`(doc, error)` -- a file that exists but does not parse is *neither*.\n\n        `_json` raises on a truncated or corrupt document, which is right for an\n        artifact the pipeline wrote and never half-writes. `state.json` is\n        different: a live loop is writing it while the browser polls it, and a\n        process killed mid-write must not turn every page of the viewer into a\n        500. So the two failures are told apart here -- `(None, None)` is \"not\n        built yet\", `(None, \"...\")` is \"built, and unreadable\" -- and the caller\n        decides which HTTP status each deserves.\n        \"\"\"\n        try:\n            return self._json(path), None\n        except ValueError as e:\n            return None, f\"{path.name} is not valid JSON: {e}\"\n\n    def _text(self, path: Path) -> str | None:\n        return self._load(path, lambda p: p.read_text())\n\n    def _footer(self, path: Path) -> dict | None:\n        \"\"\"`{rows, built_at, git_sha}` from the parquet footer alone.\n\n        `meta()` describes a dozen artifacts; opening every one of them in full\n        would make the first page load read the entire tree. The row count and\n        the provenance block both live in the footer.\n        \"\"\"\n        def _read(p: Path) -> dict:\n            pf = pq.ParquetFile(p)\n            kv = (pf.schema_arrow.metadata or {}).get(META_KEY)\n            meta = json.loads(kv) if kv else {}\n            return {\"rows\": int(pf.metadata.num_rows),\n                    \"built_at\": meta.get(\"built_at\"),\n                    \"git_sha\": meta.get(\"git_sha\")}\n        return self._load(path, _read, cache_key=f\"{path}#footer\")\n\n    # -- shared helpers -----------------------------------------------------\n    @staticmethod\n    def _ts(value) -> pd.Timestamp | None:\n        \"\"\"Parse a user-supplied timestamp into ET. None passes through.\"\"\"\n        if value is None or value == \"\":\n            return None\n        ts = pd.Timestamp(value)\n        return ts.tz_localize(TZ) if ts.tz is None else ts.tz_convert(TZ)\n\n    def _slice(self, df: pd.DataFrame, start, end) -> pd.DataFrame:\n        lo, hi = self._ts(start), self._ts(end)\n        if lo is None and hi is None:\n            return df\n        return df.loc[lo:hi]\n\n    def _regimes_df(self, rung: str = \"1h\") -> pd.DataFrame | None:\n        \"\"\"The labelled table for one rung; the 1h one keeps its own name.\n\n        The filename comes from `cyclops.regime.rungs.regimes_path` rather than\n        from a format string here, so the viewer and the builder can never\n        disagree about where a rung's labels live.\n        \"\"\"\n        name = rung_paths.regimes_path(self.regimes_dir, rung).name\n        return self._parquet(self.regimes_dir / name)\n\n    def _features_df(self) -> pd.DataFrame | None:\n        return self._parquet(self.features_dir / \"nq_1h_features.parquet\")\n\n    def _memory_df(self) -> pd.DataFrame | None:\n        return self._parquet(self.memory_dir / \"nq_1h_memory.parquet\")\n\n    def _bars_df(self, rung: str = \"1h\") -> pd.DataFrame | None:\n        return self._parquet(self.processed_dir / f\"nq_{rung}.parquet\")\n\n    def _config(self, rung: str = \"1h\") -> dict | None:\n        \"\"\"One rung's frozen thresholds — `config.json`, or `config_{rung}.json`.\n\n        The path comes from `regime.rungs.config_path` for the same reason\n        `_regimes_df`'s does: the labeller and the viewer must not be able to\n        disagree about which file a rung's thresholds live in.\n        \"\"\"\n        got = self._json(rung_paths.config_path(self.regimes_dir, rung))\n        return None if got is None else got.get(\"config\", {})\n\n    def _persistence(self, rung: str = \"1h\") -> dict | None:\n        \"\"\"One rung's duration/hazard summary — `persistence{_rung}.json`.\"\"\"\n        return self._json(rung_paths.persistence_path(self.regimes_dir, rung))\n\n    # ------------------------------------------------------------- the overlay\n    def _live_bars_df(self, rung: str) -> pd.DataFrame | None:\n        return self._parquet(self.live_dir / f\"bars_{rung}.parquet\")\n\n    def _live_labels_df(self, rung: str) -> pd.DataFrame | None:\n        return self._parquet(self.live_dir / f\"labels_{rung}.parquet\")\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def _overlay(\n        batch: pd.DataFrame | None, live: pd.DataFrame | None\n    ) -> tuple[pd.DataFrame | None, pd.Timestamp | None]:\n        \"\"\"`(batch + the live rows past it, the first live stamp)`.\n\n        The join rule is one line and it is the whole contract: a live row is\n        served only when its `ts_close` is **strictly after** the batch table's\n        last one. At the boundary the batch copy wins, for the same reason\n        `live.tail.new_1m` lets the batch 1m copy win — the nightly artifact is\n        the reconciled, roll-adjusted, HMM-labelled series and the live one is at\n        best a less complete copy of the same bar.\n\n        The live frame is reindexed onto the batch frame's columns, so a column\n        the live path does not publish (every HMM column, every state-memory\n        column, the rule inputs) arrives as null rather than as a missing key.\n\n        A batch table that does not exist stays absent: the overlay is a *tail*\n        on the record, and a viewer showing one session of live bars with no\n        history behind it would be a different screen, not a fresher one. Both\n        return values are `(batch, None)` in that case, and the caller's usual\n        \"not built yet\" path fires.\n        \"\"\"\n        if batch is None or len(batch) == 0:\n            return batch, None\n        if live is None or len(live) == 0:\n            return batch, None\n        tail = live[live.index > batch.index[-1]]\n        if le","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"persistedOutputPath":"/Users/vivekgade/.claude/projects/-private-var-folders-fj-tl69n2xd067clrqbx31xtrtr0000gn-T-t13-6o4o32at-build-repo--lattice-rebuilds-t13-cyclops-b1-19c0ae/25e55146-d7ad-4b00-9b04-b33f1b7f87c0/tool-results/bzsich2i3.txt","persistedOutputSize":173775},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01F1W8P2EAknpSdYGw73XdZz","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"subcommandResults"}}]}
